\section{A Match Is Incomplete Until the Operands Fit}

Here is a teaching reconstruction of an ADDI selector decision, not a literal VADL or LLVM rule. The IR operation is an ordinary 32-bit addition with a constant operand; only legal destinations for a real result are considered.

\begin{verbatim}
match: result32 = add32(source32, K)
if -2048 <= signed_value_32(K) <= 2047:
    emit ADDI(result_reg, source_reg, low12(K))
    # Selector checks the value before the encoder truncates it.
else:
    materialize K and use register ADD if legal
    # One possible fallback, not the only valid sequence.

K = -2048 -> ADDI with immediate 0x800
K = +2048 -> not this ADDI; the same bits decode as -2048
\end{verbatim}

The test is on the mathematical value represented by the IR constant, not on whether its low 12 bits happen to fit. The selector must also carry the source operation's width and any defined exceptional or flag effects; replacing a wider add by ADDI merely because a particular constant fits can otherwise discard behavior. If a spec revision widens the immediate, the change affects this predicate, the extraction used by the simulator, and emission. Compare the selected machine words against an independent encoding and execute them with boundary register values. To test any alternative match or more profitable sequence, compare live results and side effects, not just whether the output is shorter. Scheduling choices or instruction costs may change what is profitable without changing what is legal.

\section{Separate Pattern Coverage from End-to-End Results}

Testing direct patterns can expose a width mistake even if a backend compiles small programs. Testing fallback paths can reveal that a legal operation has no implemented lowering. Section~5.4 reports that the prototype completed four of 22 Embench programs using LLVM 10 and unoptimized settings; unsupported selection patterns were a major reason for failures, but linking and execution failures also occurred. Only the completed programs entered the reported cycle comparison, which used the generated cycle-accurate simulator. This demonstrates a backend pipeline for those cases, not comprehensive pattern coverage, production optimization, or current performance.
